  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThis approach builds upon the current optimization framework but implements crucial improvements in three key areas to better handle the non-convexity and large search space of the problem. The central idea is to use a gradient-based optimization via a smooth surrogate of the original objective, which simplifies the optimization landscape while maintaining fidelity to the true problem. This surrogate guides the search towards feasible regions with lower correlation peaks. Additionally, we introduce an adaptive block-neldermead crossing strategy and a two-tier repair mechanism - this combination increases the efficiency of local search and improves constraint satisfaction. The limitations include:\n- The gradient methodology might not escape complex local minima, but the Nelder-Mead crossing mitigates this\n- The problem remains challenging due to high dimensionality (560 features)\n- Initial conditions significantly influence convergence\n\nKey justifications:\n1. Smooth Surrogate Gradient: Approximates the combinatorial objective with a smooth convex function for gradient-based optimization\n2. Block-NelderMead Strategy: Uses integrated local refinement to balance exploration and exploitation\n3. Tiered Repair: Two repair levels ensure domain feasibility and optimality\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211 h[i] = n/2 (n=560)\n- Surrogate Formulation: \n  - Smooth approximation: f(h) = exp(-\u03b1(max_correlation - shifted_center))^\u03b2\n  - Gradient: \u2207f = (scaled gradient of the smooth approximation)\n- Nelder-Mead Method specifics:\n  - Initial simplex: `2*random_sample` + (1 - 2*random_sample) * current_point\n  - Scaled centroid application (scaling factor 0.5)\n- Two-Tier Repair:\n  - Level 1: Simple clipping repair\n  - Level 2: Iterative adjustment to meet integral constraint\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nInput: `run(seed=42, budget_s=1100)`\nOutput: `(h_values, c5_bound, n_points=560)`\n\nSteps:\n1. Initialization:\n- n=560 (dimension)\n- Set initial solution h0 = initial_h_values if available, else homogeneous array\n- Define objective via correlation\n- Set up optimization history\n\n2. Optimization Procedure:\n- Apply SmoothedGradient function using early stopping\n- For each phase:\n  a. Formulate gradient constraints for smooth surrogate\n  b. Run NelderMead with simplex size=min(n,100)\n  c. Apply TieredRepair after each phase\n  d. Local refinement via gradient-based minimization of the surrogate\n  e. Early termination if performance plateaus\n\n3. Detailed Operations:\n\nSmoothGradient function with cross-validation on:\n- Learning rate (\u03b7): 0.01 - 0.1\n- Alpha (\u03b1): 5 - 10\n\nGradient calculation:\n- Uses finite differences (\u03b5=1e-4)\n- Projects onto valid region pre-grad calculation\n\n4. Search Choices:\n- Focal Points Selection (alpha_r method): \n  - Gradient-aware top 50% and variance-based top 5%\n- Reference Set: \n  - Size = ceil(0.05*n)\n  - Fitted to current solution via projection\n\n5. Budget Handling:\n- Dominant time: Gradient computation (O(n^2))\n- Schedule: \n  - Tiered optimization (166 s)\n  - Block processing (233 s)\n  - Nelder-Mead (222 s)\n- Adaptive allocation based on gradient peak detection\n\n6. Validation:\n- Final candidate requires: \n  a. Integral check via direct L1 projection\n  b. Value check via clamping to [0,1]\n  c. Strong-gradient plateau check (\u03b5=1e-5)\nOutput: `(best_h, best_c5, n)`",
